## What Swarm actually is

It is a mode of the daemon you are already running — nothing to install.

{title="the whole of day zero"}
```bash
docker swarm init
docker swarm join --token <worker-token> <manager-ip>:2377
docker stack deploy -c compose.yml myapp
```

What that turns on, from [the Docker
documentation](https://docs.docker.com/engine/swarm/):

- **A declarative service model.** You state replica counts; the manager
  reconciles toward them and reschedules tasks when nodes go away.
- **Overlay networking** across hosts, with automatic address assignment, plus
  internal DNS and load balancing between services.
- **A [routing mesh](https://docs.docker.com/engine/swarm/ingress/)** — every
  node accepts connections on a published port and forwards to a node running
  the task, "even if there's no task running on the node".
- **[Rolling updates](https://docs.docker.com/engine/swarm/services/)** with
  `--update-parallelism`, `--update-delay`, and both manual and automatic
  rollback on a failure ratio.
- **[Secrets](https://docs.docker.com/engine/swarm/secrets/)** stored in the
  encrypted raft log, distributed over mutual TLS, and mounted into an
  in-memory filesystem at `/run/secrets` — never as environment variables,
  which the docs call "a conscious decision, because environment variables can
  unintentionally be leaked between containers". Up to 500 kB each.
- **Mutual TLS between nodes by default**, with no flag to remember.

That is not a thinned-out Kubernetes for beginners; it is the set of things
that matter on a small fleet, in roughly a dozen CLI verbs.

And it is maintained. Docker Engine 29.0.0 shipped on 10 November 2025 and
29.7.1 on 31 July 2026, with [Swarm fixes throughout that
series](https://docs.docker.com/engine/release-notes/29/): raft snapshot
corruption with large swarm state (29.5) · stale VIP DNS records left behind
during rolling updates (29.5) · service updates failing on an existing VIP
alias (29.6) · tasks rejected when an image could not be pulled but was present
locally (29.6) · a daemon panic removing ingress ports after a failed listener
bind (29.7). The most recent commit on
[moby/swarmkit](https://github.com/moby/swarmkit) is dated 4 August 2026.

The counterweight is the same evidence read closely: the last week of swarmkit
commits is Go 1.25 idioms, linter fixes, a removed dependency and a corrected
godoc link — a codebase kept correct, not extended. **Swarm is
maintained, and it is not being grown.** Mirantis, which acquired Docker
Enterprise in November 2019, dropped its two-year Swarm support end date in
[February
2020](https://www.mirantis.com/blog/mirantis-will-continue-to-support-and-develop-docker-swarm/)
— "our customers want continued support of Swarm without an implied end date" —
and its [product page](https://www.mirantis.com/software/swarm/) still promises
to "maintain, enhance, fully test, and support Swarm" with a dedicated team. I
weight a bug fix from last month far above a commitment from 2020.

::: note
**Classic Swarm was a different product.** The standalone orchestrator with an
external key/value store was [deprecated in Engine v20.10 and removed in
v23.0](https://docs.docker.com/engine/deprecated/). Swarm *mode*, built into
the engine, is not on that page — the documentation's own line is "Do
not confuse Docker Swarm mode with Docker Classic Swarm which is no longer
actively developed." Almost every "Swarm is dead" claim I have read is the
removed product reported as live.
:::

## Should you use it

**Probably not, if** you have one application on one box: an orchestrator is
overhead you have not earned. I run [AthletOS on a single FreeBSD
machine](/blog/athletos-freebsd/) for about €7 a month.

**Maybe, if** you have three to ten machines running stateless HTTP services,
one team, compose files you already wrote, and nobody whose job title contains
the word platform — such a person will build Kubernetes eventually anyway.

**Probably not, if** several teams must deploy independently without reading
each other's secrets. Swarm has no namespace, and everything you would build
that isolation out of is missing rather than inconvenient.

**No, if** you came here for a database operator, a certificate controller, a
log pipeline or anything else you expected to install rather than write.

## The comparison

A capability inventory, not a benchmark, with measurements only where I have
them.

| | Swarm | The k3s kit |
|---|---|---|
| Install | a mode of a daemon you run · `docker swarm init` | node layer, then 9 Kustomizations and 8 Helm releases |
| Scheduling, restart, rolling update, rollback | yes | yes |
| Service discovery, L4 balancing | routing mesh, built in | Service, kube-proxy |
| HTTP routing: host, path, TLS | **not in the mesh** — L4 only; run a proxy in front | Gateway API + cert-manager, in the kit |
| Secrets | encrypted raft log, in-memory mount, 500 kB, **immutable** | Secret objects, SOPS + age encrypted in git |
| Autoscaling | **none** — replicated or global, scaled by hand | cluster autoscaler shipped; HPA available |
| Extension model | **none** | CRDs — the kit installs **81** before a tenant exists |
| Admission / policy | **none** | admission policy; the kit refuses a hostname claim with one |
| Tenancy primitive | overlay network attachment | namespace + quota + limits + NetworkPolicy + pod security + RBAC |
| Off-the-shelf components | write the compose file yourself | [Artifact Hub](https://artifacthub.io) lists **21,340** packages |
| Control-plane quorum | raft · 3 managers tolerate 1 loss | etcd · 3 members tolerate 1 loss |
| Manifest format | legacy Compose v3, via `docker stack deploy` | Kubernetes YAML |
| Platform footprint at rest | inside `dockerd` · **not measured here** | **4,526 Mi · 194m** across 43 pods, measured |
| Size of the thing you maintain | a compose file and a join token | 122 tracked files · 27,182 lines |

The bolded absences are the argument; the bolded numbers are the price of not
having them.

One row people get wrong in Swarm's favour: **the routing mesh is layer 4.** No
host or path routing, no TLS termination. The documentation's own answer is
HAProxy in front, so certificates, virtual hosts and redirects are yours to
build and operate.

## What a compose file buys you

The file you already use in development is *nearly* the deployment artefact:
services, images, environment, networks, volumes and dependencies all carry
over, and you add a `deploy:` block with a replica count and an update policy.

But `docker stack deploy` consumes [the legacy Compose file version
3](https://docs.docker.com/engine/swarm/stack-deploy/), not the current
[Compose Specification](https://docs.docker.com/reference/compose-file/legacy-versions/)
that merged 2.x and 3.x and that every other tool now reads. The legacy formats
are documented as "no longer being actively maintained". `build` is ignored
outright — the deploy prints `Ignoring unsupported options: build` — so you push
images to a registry first, and the registry is a thing you now run or pay for.

"Your compose file is already a Swarm stack" is true in the way that matters,
false in the details. Budget an afternoon, not a week, and budget the registry
properly.

## What you give up

**There is no extension mechanism**, and everything else follows from it. The
CustomResourceDefinition is why the operator ecosystem exists; the kit installs
81 CRDs before a tenant exists, which is both the cost of Kubernetes and the
reason anything off-the-shelf works. Every stateful thing you would have handed
to an operator — failover, backup, restore, major-version upgrade — is yours.

**There is no admission stage.** The kit refuses a route claiming another
tenant's hostname, or declaring none, at admission. In Swarm there is nowhere
to put a rule of that shape; you check it in CI and hope nobody applies by hand.

**There is no autoscaling.** Services are replicated or global. Capacity
follows demand when a human types `docker service scale`.

**There is no namespace.** Isolation is which overlay network a workload
attaches to; the documentation describes attachment and encryption and nothing
else — no per-tenant budget, no policy layer. Fine for one team; not a
multi-tenancy model.

**There are fewer people to ask.** Artifact Hub lists 21,340 packages,
essentially all of them Kubernetes. The 2025 Stack Overflow developer survey
put Docker at 71.1% and Kubernetes at 28.5% — and did not list Swarm at all,
which is evidence about its authors' assumptions, not about the install base.
But when something breaks at 02:00, the number of people who have seen your
error message before is much smaller.

## What I would test next

I have run the k3s kit on real hardware four times and Swarm not at this scale,
so that table's empty cell stays empty rather than getting a plausible number.
The next jobs:

- **Measure Swarm's footprint at rest.** It runs inside `dockerd` rather than
  as a fleet of control-plane pods, so it is structurally much smaller than
  4,526 Mi. *How much* smaller is a number someone has to measure, and nobody
  quoting one has.
- **Kill a manager, then kill an etcd member.** The kit's HA control plane
  formed and was healthy; watching the survivors carry on is the test I have
  run on neither side.
- **The install base and the five-year question stay open.** Nobody publishes
  install numbers, so every claim about Swarm's popularity is somebody's
  impression, and viability in 2031 is a guess — including for anyone citing
  the 2020 commitment.

## Three bills that follow you across

I collected forty-nine lessons building the k3s kit. Uncomfortably few care
which orchestrator you chose: Swarm keeps you paying two of these and genuinely
retires the third.

## Lesson one: quorum arithmetic is the same arithmetic

I destroyed a cluster by shrinking its control plane. Infrastructure-as-code
manages servers, not etcd membership, so three server resources becoming one is
a diff applied without comment — and, to etcd, the loss of two of three voters.
Majority never returns, and the failure surfaces as a provisioner timeout, exit
status 124.

Swarm is raft too, and [the admin
guide](https://docs.docker.com/engine/swarm/admin_guide/) publishes the
identical table: 3 managers tolerate 1 failure, 5 tolerate 2, 7 tolerate 3.
Growing is safe, shrinking is not, and past quorum "swarm nodes cannot be
added, updated, or removed, and new or existing tasks cannot be started,
stopped, moved, or updated." Swarm's one mercy is
`docker swarm init --force-new-cluster`, a documented recovery path from a
surviving manager.

> A replica count that a consensus protocol depends on is not a number in your
> configuration. It is cluster state that happens to be spelled the same way.

## Lesson two: no namespace means no generator

Across a mature multi-tenant cluster, guarantees emitted by the path that
*creates* a tenant were present in over ninety percent of namespaces; written
down as recommendations instead, in **none**. The axis is not enforcement
versus suggestion — it is in the generator versus not in the generator.

Swarm has no namespace, so there is nothing for a generator to emit. Every
guarantee you want — a budget, an isolation rule, a backup, a disruption policy
— lives in prose, a runbook, or a script somebody remembers to run: the "none"
column by construction, invisible until the second tenant, which is exactly
when it is expensive to fix.

## Lesson three: fewer things create resources behind your back

`tofu destroy` walks its own state. A Kubernetes cluster also creates provider
resources *at runtime* — servers from the autoscaler, a load balancer per
`LoadBalancer` Service, a volume per claim — which are not in that state, so
destroy reports success while they keep running and keep billing. Nor are they
inert: an autoscaler-created server stays attached to the private network, so
destroying the *network* fails, naming the network rather than the node holding
it. Three CSI volumes and one cloud-controller load balancer had to go by hand
before the verification cluster would `destroy`.

Swarm mints far less on your behalf: no cloud controller, no CSI driver, no
autoscaler.

**This is the real shape of the Swarm trade.** Not that Swarm is simpler to
learn, although it is, but that fewer components act on your behalf — fewer
things doing something correct that you did not ask for and cannot see.

## The verdict

Where that maybe-if holds, Swarm is not the lesser choice. It is the correct
one, and the k3s kit is roughly 4.5 GiB of memory and 27,000 lines of
repository solving problems you do not have.

**The answer flips on team shape before it flips on fleet size.** Twenty
machines running one team's stateless services is still comfortably Swarm. Four
machines shared by three teams who must not read each other's secrets is not,
at any size: the thing you need is the namespace and everything generated with
it, and that is not a feature Swarm is missing but a model Swarm does not have.

Short of that, the honest cost of Swarm is not technical. It is that you are
increasingly alone: fewer components, fewer answers, and a maintenance signal
that says *kept correct* rather than *being grown*. For a small fleet that is a
defensible trade and I would make it. For a platform you expect to hand to
someone else in three years, I would not.

And if even that is more machinery than your problem deserves, the answer is
not a smaller orchestrator. It is [no orchestrator at
all](/blog/three-freebsd-boxes-instead-of-k3s/).
